#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158391} :activity/id #uuid "c2c46d67-88f2-5cb5-ad0b-e155cb945332" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050450 :topic/id #uuid "49ef522d-8ba6-502a-8a64-c19ff9a6b681" :topic/math-academy-id 2818} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158467 :tutorial/content "For a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \nwe know that if $A$ is diagonalizable and has eigenvectors ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ with the corresponding eigenvalues ${\\color{black}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{black}\\lambda_2},$ respectively, then the general solution formula is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nBut what happens if $A$ has a single repeated eigenvalue, $\\lambda?$ It depends on the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\n- We say that $\\lambda$ is a **complete** eigenvalue if there are two linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\n- We say that $\\lambda$ is a **defective** (or **incomplete**) eigenvalue if there is only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v$ corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\nThis leads to two distinct cases:\n\n- *The Complete Case.* If $\\lambda$ is a *complete* eigenvalue, then there are two linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to $\\lambda,$ and we can use the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda} t}\n$$\n\n- *The Defective Case.* If $\\lambda$ is a *defective* eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v.$ We cannot use the usual formula because there are not enough eigenvectors.\n\nInstead, we must find a *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf w,$ which is a vector that satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is then given by the formula: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, )\n$$\n\nWe'll explain how and why this solution comes about at the end of the lesson.\n\nNow, let's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "76c4a128-aa18-5cb4-a460-0811386673d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4281 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fb2bbd4a-aeae-553c-ac02-210278766cbb" :step/math-academy-id 550835 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158392}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157073 :question/id #uuid "af260af2-fd05-54bc-8f6f-a5612cb922db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7048"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049852}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557308}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"} :step/id #uuid "59fd701c-e0be-5da0-8006-b206fcd15119" :step/math-academy-id 550836 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157074 :question/id #uuid "f1eaa2b8-544c-5217-82a5-8ec0a311c5a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7049"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530517}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"} :step/id #uuid "bf1cdfb1-e3d1-56f0-b6a6-775a91e83782" :step/math-academy-id 550839 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157075 :question/id #uuid "38f7a261-b58c-5ce1-ab77-6bfb5ce40b19" :question/math-academy-id "e-7050"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050098}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530529}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"} :step/id #uuid "4b9a873f-47e9-56dd-84a0-2bf953ccd698" :step/math-academy-id 550842 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158395}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158468 :tutorial/content "When solving the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \n\nin the case where $A$ has a single, defective eigenvalue $\\lambda$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the first fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1(t) = \\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t},\n$$\n\nas usual, and the second fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and $\\mathbf w$ is a vector that satisfies $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nWe can see that the second fundamental solution works by substituting $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{′}(t) & =A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}] & =A(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}) \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Avt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v+\\lambda w & =Aw \\\\ v & =Aw-\\lambda w \\\\ v & =(A-\\lambda I)w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we conclude that $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ works as a second fundamental solution provided that $\\mathbf w$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{v}  =  (A - \\lambda I) \\mathbf{w}.$\n\n**Note:**\nBy linearity, any linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ is also a solution of the system. Moreover, it turns out, every solution can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t).$ Later in the course, we’ll see why certain particular solutions can generate the entire solution set." :tutorial/id #uuid "044881e6-feac-543e-b223-ea1a1c9da634" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4282 :tutorial/title "Justification for the Solution Formula in the Defective Case"} :step/id #uuid "37b8ee3e-0296-5c88-ad97-3614e478781f" :step/math-academy-id 550845}] :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Repeated Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157476}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557379} :activity/id #uuid "69e8d858-bc7b-50b8-b9ca-21c046adc840" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050450 :topic/id #uuid "49ef522d-8ba6-502a-8a64-c19ff9a6b681" :topic/math-academy-id 2818} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66613f28-b688-5af5-87c4-a12f46f9bd0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399140} {:answer/id #uuid "b467a51e-f9be-5bed-89bc-7ff012c894de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399141} {:answer/id #uuid "149698b5-3361-5f52-a32c-6ed656acfe8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399142} {:answer/id #uuid "748a5bc0-46b6-5698-846f-66d6952f985d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399143} {:answer/id #uuid "99a4d6e3-caf9-544f-ba67-8f950954be82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399140} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2967834-56c7-54af-a045-1eb688f37565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8179a1bc-9dd2-5c9c-a7dd-3de3a7cd250c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80917" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+2{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "292b00bf-bb40-5de2-a2ae-9b94332bbb33" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557400}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1570ae2-7522-574c-b09e-cc0720cebf4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398715} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4fc918-4e9b-5f09-b20e-cf2be22cf7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398716} {:answer/id #uuid "ab281b2e-5a53-5672-8da4-e676a03d7e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398717} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0ec4cd-48eb-5345-9138-6da28aeb3e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398718} {:answer/id #uuid "56280ebc-3607-5e92-83e5-9a26a408be13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398719}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398718} :answer-field/id #uuid "12200322-ef19-5f83-936e-a28151c6b492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "17c5a462-c29c-5b82-b891-ee8c95c1220f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326236" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1-(-1) & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1-(-1)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "4371b504-a799-50b5-a82a-215dffd22710" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557374}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557374 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ca2d32a-4c04-50ff-91f0-8046260807a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300584} {:answer/id #uuid "d2289300-75a9-5b84-84a6-d44ebc08ff8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301009} {:answer/id #uuid "e9eb9a48-bf69-59f2-b28c-d145d8739241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301120} {:answer/id #uuid "f182e5a2-a9f3-573e-9415-618ee51f716c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301154} {:answer/id #uuid "f8829e7a-583b-577a-8af0-a6321d9e75f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301154} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8123425-b2ad-5be3-af0f-d40f08f7cc7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6648c16d-423d-571d-89b3-28f10f5f61b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-326238" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3-(-3) & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3-(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-6{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "863071d9-8226-571f-9257-700dcf82f4a3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e08605f-fa3a-569b-af1b-d45a70992915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556772} {:answer/id #uuid "d5cefb37-7da0-53cb-8ec1-47dc88f4f3e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556950} {:answer/id #uuid "da268bf5-cb3d-56a1-95c2-03e31ddbd921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556971} {:answer/id #uuid "f787f3d0-ca6e-5982-8896-c3df5a7de145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557043} {:answer/id #uuid "fd03625b-a11f-58bd-a340-4e7487dfc2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556950} :answer-field/id #uuid "45a64cfe-8191-5b79-aaff-a5627b1f6695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb79f743-bef1-5e9c-b5b3-1f6ceebb3420" :question/math-academy-id "q-80911" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8f062582-b92a-5ca2-a69b-5fcb27e36a6a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557400 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42865a1d-e7dd-5742-a905-26015fcbdb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349050} {:answer/id #uuid "690b864e-4301-536f-abb4-d98819a36a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349415} {:answer/id #uuid "96e30ebf-f753-5f2c-bfda-1443e5566cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349801} {:answer/id #uuid "9a777a2e-88d9-547b-9226-0f78646dae73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349824} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bfb469-6607-5051-a5c7-d072ad71b3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350054}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349801} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaac654c-f8f2-5836-8ce9-667c860921c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "594f8d9b-4d22-56cb-9100-eca1eb140e4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80927" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2+2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "de1c04b6-3944-5a50-8bfe-e60e82727aab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557358}}] :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Repeated Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556412}]}
